B.aj9=s(["Assess every neonate born in or brought to a health facility for presence of sepsis, at admission and during hospital stay, by looking for red and yellow flag signs and risk factors"],t.s)
B.agQ=s(["Shock","Bleeding from multiple sites","Sclerema \u2013 hardened skin over cheeks / thighs",u.gN,"If age of baby is less than 72 hours and mother has foul-smelling liquor or chorioamnionitis"],t.s)
B.aab=s(["Seizures*","Refusal to feed",u.bW,"Fast breathing (RR >60/min)","Floppiness","Lethargy","Feed intolerance","New or increased apneic episodes",u.f],t.s)
B.adW=s(["Any of the maternal risk factors: If age of baby is less than 3 days (72 hrs) and mother has >3 PV examinations during labour; Rupture of membranes >24 hrs; pPROM; Urinary tract infection; Diarrhea; Fever >38\xb0C"],t.s)
B.a7K=s(["START TREATMENT AND INVESTIGATE","Any RED flag is present","Any two YELLOW flags (signs or maternal risk factors) are present","One YELLOW flag (sign or maternal risk factor) is present AND baby\u2019s gestation at birth is \u226432 weeks or birth weight <1500 g","Admit in the NICU / SNCU","Obtain blood sample for culture and sensitivity (even if baby has received an antibiotic before admission)","Start empirical antibiotics as per local/unit policy, pending reports","Provide supportive care: thermal care, fluids & feeds, glucose monitoring, respiratory & circulatory support","Do labs as indicated (X-ray, CBC, platelets, RBS, electrolytes, renal function)","Perform lumbar puncture (LP) for CSF analysis when baby is hemodynamically stable"],t.s)
B.ae0=s(["Neonatal seizure always warrants urgent evaluation for cause (sepsis / meningitis, hypoglycaemia). Keep a low threshold to start antibiotics and perform LP."],t.s)
B.a9f=s(["Stop antibiotics","Keep under observation till blood culture is reported as sterile after 48 hrs of incubation"],t.s)
B.aak=s(["Continue antibiotics",u.ev],t.s)
B.ab4=s(["Upgrade antibiotics as per antibiotic local/unit policy",u.ev],t.s)
B.aaf=s(["If antibiotics were continued, review at 5 days. If the baby is now well, the 48-h blood culture is sterile and CSF is normal, stop antibiotics."],t.s)
B.a7y=s(["A sterile blood culture at 24\u201348 h after starting antibiotics supports early stopping of antibiotics."],t.s)
B.ae6=s(["Start antibiotics within 1 hour if high probability of sepsis is present","Draw blood culture before the first antibiotic dose","Review antibiotics at 48 h against culture, CRP and clinical course","Stop antibiotics if blood culture is sterile and the baby has recovered","Use serial observation and look for non-infective causes of illness"],t.s)
B.abH=s(["Start antibiotics without an indication \u2013 clinical signs in neonates are non-specific","Continue antibiotics for a sterile culture in a baby who has recovered","Act on a positive CRP / procalcitonin in isolation \u2013 they are false positive in many non-infective conditions; use to rule out sepsis","Delay LP when it is indicated once the baby is stable"],t.s)
B.agT=s(["Blood culture before first antibiotic dose (%): Sepsis episodes with blood culture drawn before the first antibiotic dose / Sepsis episodes in which antibiotics were started \xd7100","Antibiotic initiation within 1 hour (%): Neonates with high probability of sepsis who received the first antibiotic dose within 1 hour of identification / All neonates identified as having high probability of sepsis \xd7100","Antibiotic exposure during admission (%): Neonates admitted during the reporting period who received at least one dose of a systemic antibiotic during their stay / All neonates admitted during the same reporting period \xd7100"],t.s)
B.a7t=s(["PREVENT SEPSIS BY ENSURING HAND HYGIENE, ASEPSIS DURING PROCEDURES AND DILIGENT HOUSEKEEPING"],t.s)
B.aiu=s(["RELATED INFORMATION","Hand hygiene","youtu.be/lkp5BXzPYBA","Blood culture","youtu.be/6pQBzt-EZN8","Lumbar puncture","youtu.be/6-h4xUIrMCg"],t.s)
B.aiM=s(["ABBREVIATIONS","CBC: Complete blood count","LP: Lumbar puncture","RBS: Random blood sugar","CRP: C-reactive protein","NICU: Neonatal intensive care unit","SAS: Silverman-Andersen score","CSF: Cerebrospinal fluid","pPROM: Preterm premature rupture of membranes","SNCU: Special newborn care unit"],t.s)
B.aaV=s(["REFERENCES","1. The Young Infants Clinical Signs Study Group. Clinical signs that predict severe illness in children under age 2 months: a multicentre study. Lancet. 2008;371(9607):135\u201342.","2. Neonatal infection: antibiotics for prevention and treatment. London: National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE); 2021 Apr 20. (NICE Guideline, No.195). Available from: https://","www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng195 Last access on 20/04/2022"],t.s)
B.al5=new A.by(B.amj,[B.aj9,B.agQ,B.aab,B.adW,B.a7K,B.tw,B.ae0,B.a9f,B.aak,B.ab4,B.aaf,B.a7y,B.tC,B.ae6,B.abH,B.agT,B.a7t,B.aiu,B.aiM,B.aaV],t.VJ)
B.agL=s(["Heart rate of 160 beats per minute","Respiratory rate of 68 breaths per minute","Capillary refill time >2 seconds","Axillary temperature <36.5\xb0C"],t.s)
B.a1b=new A.bX("rd_mcq_1","Which clinical sign is diagnostic of respiratory distress in a newborn according to the Standard Treatment Workflow (STW)?",B.agL,1,"The guidelines define neonatal respiratory distress by the presence of ANY ONE of four criteria: Respiratory rate \u226560/min, chest retractions, nasal flaring, or grunting.","Diagnosis",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), RESPIRATORY DISTRESS; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q1')
B.aeQ=s(["91\u201394%","90\u201392%","91\u201395%","95\u201397%"],t.s)
B.a1o=new A.bX("rd_mcq_2","What is the target peripheral oxygen saturation (SpO\u2082) range for neonates receiving supplemental oxygen or CPAP support?",B.aeQ,2,"Guidelines mandate using air-oxygen blenders and continuous pulse oximetry to strictly maintain and target SpO\u2082 between 91\u201395% for all neonates receiving oxygen or CPAP.","Oxygen target",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DOs; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q2')
B.aef=s([">75%",">80%",">85%",">90%"],t.s)
B.a1r=new A.bX("rd_mcq_3","What is the Key Performance Indicator (KPI) target for timely initiation of CPAP (within 30 minutes of identification) in eligible neonates?",B.aef,3,"The KPI target for timely CPAP initiation\u2014starting CPAP within 30 minutes of identifying an eligible neonate\u2014is >90%.","CPAP initiation KPI",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), KEY PERFORMANCE INDICATORS (KPIs); MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q3')
B.aj8=s(["FiO\u2082 >0.25","FiO\u2082 >0.30","FiO\u2082 >0.40","FiO\u2082 >0.50"],t.s)
B.a1Q=new A.bX("rd_mcq_4","In a preterm neonate <34 weeks gestational age receiving CPAP, what FiO\u2082 threshold on CPAP \u22656 cm H\u2082O indicates early rescue surfactant therapy?",B.aj8,1,"Early rescue surfactant is indicated in a preterm neonate <34 weeks with suspected worsening RDS who requires an FiO\u2082 >0.30 on CPAP \u22656 cm H\u2082O to maintain SpO\u2082 at 91\u201395%.","Early rescue surfactant",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), EARLY RESCUE SURFACTANT; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q4')
B.add=s(["Continuous pulse oximetry monitoring","Silverman\u2013Andersen Score (SAS) assessment","Routine CBC/CRP, chest X-ray, or arterial blood gas","Thermal protection and TABC stabilization"],t.s)
B.a1g=new A.bX("rd_mcq_5",'Which of the following routine diagnostic investigations is explicitly listed as a "DON\'T" in uncomplicated mild respiratory distress?',B.add,2,'The guidelines explicitly state: "Do NOT order routine CBC/CRP, chest X-ray or blood gas in uncomplicated mild RD" to avoid unnecessary procedures.',"Avoid routine investigations",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DON\u2019Ts; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q5')
B.ab9=s(["Respiratory rate, heart rate, capillary refill time, grunting, and skin color","Upper chest movement, lower chest retractions, xiphoid retractions, nares dilatation, and expiratory grunt","Nasal flaring, stridor, wheezing, abdominal distension, and cyanosis","Apnea duration, heart rate, head bobbing, intercostal retractions, and SpO\u2082"],t.s)
B.a1B=new A.bX("rd_mcq_6","Which five physical parameters are evaluated as part of the Silverman\u2013Andersen Score (SAS)?",B.ab9,1,"The Silverman\u2013Andersen Score evaluates 5 parameters, each scored from 0 to 2 (total 0\u201310): Upper chest movement (synchronized, lag, or see-saw), lower chest retractions, xiphoid retractions, nares dilatation, and expiratory grunt. An SAS \u22643 indicates mild RD, while \u22654 indicates moderate-to-severe RD.","Silverman\u2013Andersen Score",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), SILVERMAN\u2013ANDERSEN SCORE; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q6')
B.adX=s(["All term neonates presenting with transient tachypnea","Preterm infants <34 weeks gestation requiring respiratory support","Only preterm infants who fail CPAP and require mechanical ventilation","All preterm neonates <34 weeks"],t.s)
B.a1N=new A.bX("rd_mcq_7","According to the STW algorithm, which group of neonates should receive caffeine citrate therapy as part of their initial management?",B.adX,1,"Caffeine citrate is indicated for all preterm infants <34 weeks gestational age who require respiratory support (such as CPAP or oxygen) as per protocol.","Caffeine",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), START CPAP; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q7')
B.aaD=s(["BW \u22641000 g","BW \u22641500 g","BW \u22641800 g","BW \u22642500 g"],t.s)
B.a25=new A.bX("rd_mcq_8","When gestational age (GA) is uncertain, what operational surrogate birth weight (BW) cutoff may be used to guide management in the STW algorithm?",B.aaD,2,'The algorithm explicitly notes: "If GA is uncertain, BW \u22641800 g may be used as an operational surrogate" to decide eligibility for CPAP and caffeine therapy.',"Uncertain gestational age",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), START CPAP; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q8')
B.afJ=s(["Reassess within 15\u201330 min of starting/changing support, then at least 1 hourly","Reassess every 4 hours","Reassess only once per shift (every 12 hours)","Reassess only if the pulse oximeter alarm sounds"],t.s)
B.a27=new A.bX("rd_mcq_9","What is the mandatory clinical reassessment frequency for a neonate placed on supplemental oxygen or CPAP?",B.afJ,0,"Guidelines mandate reassessing clinical status, SAS, and FiO\u2082 requirement within 15\u201330 minutes of starting or changing support, and at least 1 hourly while on respiratory support (and immediately if any worsening occurs).","Reassessment",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), MONITORING; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q9')
B.aej=s(["2 hours","6 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],t.s)
B.a1m=new A.bX("rd_mcq_10","Following a trial off CPAP when stable on CPAP 5 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.21\u20130.25, how long must continuous pulse oximetry monitoring be maintained?",B.aej,3,"After trialing off CPAP when stable on CPAP 5 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.21\u20130.25, clinicians must monitor SpO\u2082 for 24 hours after stopping.","Weaning CPAP",null,'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), WEAN WHEN STABLE; MCQs RD.docx Section 1 Q10')
B.adE=s([B.a1b,B.a1o,B.a1r,B.a1Q,B.a1g,B.a1B,B.a1N,B.a25,B.a27,B.a1m],t.tw)
B.adw=s(["Initiate low-flow nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5 L/min","Start CPAP at 5\u20136 cm H\u2082O with blended oxygen titrated to SpO\u2082 91\u201395%","Administer prophylactic surfactant via endotracheal tube immediately","Intubate and initiate mechanical ventilation"],t.s)
B.a20=new A.bX("rd_case_1","What is the most appropriate initial respiratory management?",B.adw,1,"For any neonate with GA \u226434 weeks presenting with ANY respiratory distress (regardless of whether SAS is mild or severe), the algorithm mandates starting CPAP at 5\u20136 cm H\u2082O with blended oxygen. Low-flow nasal prongs oxygen is reserved only for GA >34 weeks with mild RD.","Preterm respiratory support","A 31-week preterm infant born in a primary care center presents with nasal flaring and mild intercostal retractions 15 minutes after birth. The Silverman\u2013Andersen Score (SAS) is calculated as 3.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), START CPAP; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q11')
B.adp=s(["Start CPAP at 7 cm H\u2082O immediately","Administer an IV bolus of caffeine citrate","Start nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5\u20131 L/min and titrate to SpO\u2082 91\u201395%","Order an urgent blood gas and chest X-ray prior to initiating oxygen"],t.s)
B.a1H=new A.bX("rd_case_2","How should oxygen therapy be initiated according to the STW algorithm?",B.adp,2,"For neonates with GA >34 weeks having mild RD (SAS \u22643) and SpO\u2082 <91%, the initial step is nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5\u20131 L/min, titrating FiO\u2082 to achieve SpO\u2082 91\u201395%. Routine lab tests or X-rays are not indicated for uncomplicated mild RD.","Term mild respiratory distress","A 38-week term newborn exhibits a respiratory rate of 66/min and mild subcostal retractions (SAS = 2). Pulse oximetry on room air reads 88%.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), NASAL PRONGS OXYGEN; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q12')
B.afV=s(["Wait for established CPAP failure before intervening","Administer early rescue surfactant promptly","Increase CPAP pressure to 10 cm H\u2082O","Wait for 2 hours to administer surfactant"],t.s)
B.a24=new A.bX("rd_case_3","What is the next best clinical action?",B.afV,1,"In preterm neonates <34 weeks with suspected worsening RDS, requiring FiO\u2082 >0.30 on CPAP \u22656 cm H\u2082O to maintain target saturation is a direct indication for early rescue surfactant, which should be given promptly before CPAP failure occurs.","Early rescue surfactant","A 29-week infant with RDS is managed on CPAP at 6 cm H\u2082O. Over the last 30 minutes, the infant's oxygen requirement has progressively escalated from FiO\u2082 0.25 to 0.35 to keep SpO\u2082within 91\u201395%. Circuit and prong placements are verified.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), EARLY RESCUE SURFACTANT; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q13')
B.ai9=s(["Increase nasal prongs oxygen flow to 4 L/min","Initiate CPAP, check prongs/circuit, and optimize pressure up to 7\u20138 cm H\u2082O","Administer routine IV fluid bolus and broad-spectrum antibiotics","Discontinue oxygen and start oral bolus feeding"],t.s)
B.a22=new A.bX("rd_case_4","What is the correct management escalation?",B.ai9,1,"When a baby on nasal prongs oxygen shows rising oxygen requirement or worsening distress (SAS \u22654), the protocol directs escalating to CPAP, checking the circuit/prongs, increasing pressure up to 7\u20138 cm H\u2082O, and titrating FiO\u2082.","Escalating respiratory support","A 36-week infant receiving nasal prongs oxygen at 1 L/min develops increasing intercostal retractions, grunting, and rising oxygen demand, with SAS increasing from 2 to 5.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), OPTIMIZE RESPIRATORY SUPPORT; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q14')
B.ahj=s(["Continue CPAP 8 cm H\u2082O and re-evaluate in 4 hours","Recognize CPAP failure, escalate support (intubate/ventilate when indicated), stabilize, and refer urgently","Reduce CPAP pressure to 4 cm H\u2082O to rest the respiratory muscles","Switch to unmonitored supplemental oxygen via nasal prongs"],t.s)
B.a1V=new A.bX("rd_case_5","What urgent action is mandated?",B.ahj,1,"Persistent hypoxaemia/rising FiO\u2082 despite optimized CPAP, recurrent apnoea, shock, or fatigue defines CPAP failure. Guidelines require escalating support, intubating/ventilating when indicated, stabilizing, and referring urgently without delay.","CPAP failure and referral","A 30-week neonate on CPAP 8 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.50 experiences recurrent severe apnoeic spells, persistent hypoxaemia (SpO\u2082 83%), and marked chest wall fatigue. Pneumothorax is ruled out by transillumination.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), CPAP FAILURE \u2014 ESCALATE PROMPTLY; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q15')
B.aeD=s(["Increase the water level in the bottle above the target PEEP line","Check for circuit air leaks, verify snug fit of binasal prongs, and check gas flow","Immediately escalate FiO\u2082 to 1.0 without checking the circuit","Increase the PEEP"],t.s)
B.a1L=new A.bX("rd_case_6","What is the most appropriate immediate step to troubleshoot this setup?",B.aeD,1,"Continuous bubbling in the generator bottle confirms delivered PEEP. If bubbling stops or distress worsens, the primary troubleshooting steps are inspecting for circuit leaks, ensuring proper sizing/seal of binasal prongs, and verifying gas flow.","Bubble CPAP troubleshooting","A staff nurse in the SNCU is setting up a bubble CPAP system for a 30-week preterm newborn with respiratory distress. After positioning the binasal prongs and starting gas flow, the nurse notices that there is no continuous bubbling in the water generator bottle and the infant's chest retractions are worsening.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DOs; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q16')
B.aad=s(["Apnea of prematurity","Sepsis","Normal physiological adaptation","Congenital pulmonary airway malformation"],t.s)
B.a1e=new A.bX("rd_case_7","According to the STW algorithm, what underlying complication should be actively considered at this stage?",B.aad,1,'In the algorithm under "NOT IMPROVING / WORSENING" (characterized by persisting/increasing SAS, SpO\u2082 <91% or rising oxygen demand, and recurrent apnoea/bradycardia), the protocol explicitly instructs clinicians to "Consider sepsis (Refer to sepsis STW)".',"Worsening respiratory distress","A 30-week preterm neonate with respiratory distress was started on Bubble CPAP at birth. At 6 hours of life, despite circuit verification, the baby exhibits persistent SAS, rising oxygen demand (FiO\u2082 increased from 0.25 to 0.45), and new-onset recurrent apnoea/bradycardia episodes.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), NOT IMPROVING / WORSENING; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q17')
B.abe=s(["Keep the baby strictly NPO on IV fluids until completely weaned off CPAP","Initiate orogastric (OG) tube feeds when appropriate, ensuring stomach decompression","Start direct oral bottle feeding immediately","Provide rapid bolus feeds via nasogastric tube without venting"],t.s)
B.a1Y=new A.bX("rd_case_8","According to the STW, what is the most appropriate feeding management strategy?",B.abe,1,"When stable on CPAP, orogastric (OG) tube feeds are indicated when appropriate, along with stomach decompression to vent swallowed air. Withholding feeds and giving IV fluids is reserved for severe RD, recurrent apnoea, poor perfusion, or abdominal concerns.","Feeding on CPAP","A 31-week preterm infant with RDS is stable on CPAP at 5 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.25, demonstrating comfortable breathing and an SAS of 2.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DOs; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q18')
